

## Open a manual
```bash
man ls          # open the manual (q to quit)
man 5 passwd    # open a specific section
ls --help       # quick built-in help
```

## Find the right command
```bash
man -f ls       # one-line description (same as: whatis ls)
man -k copy     # search all manuals by keyword
man -k '^ls'    # regex: names starting with "ls"
man -k '^ls$'   # regex: exactly "ls"
man -w ls       # where the manual file lives
```

## Search inside a manual
| Key | Action |
|-----|--------|
| `/word` | search forward |
| `n` / `N` | next / previous match |
| `Space` / `b` | page down / up |
| `q` | quit |

## Sections
| # | Contains |
|---|----------|
| 1 | Commands |
| 5 | File formats and config files |
| 7 | Overviews |
| 8 | Admin commands |

```bash
man 1 intro     # section number goes BEFORE the name
man 8 intro
```

## Gotchas
- Options and section numbers always go **before** the command: `man -f ls`, not `man ls -f`.
- `man -k` matches names and descriptions anywhere, so use `^` and `$` to cut the noise.
- `man man description` does not search inside a page. It looks for a manual named `description`.

## Quick tip
```bash
ls --help | grep "human"   # find one option fast
```
